From 169d8db583a12f579752a4a9dfbb2b7588efd293 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 23:42:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 01/15] [ffi] support tagged foreign handles

`#[ffi(rust = "path", tagged)]` declares an opaque record whose handle may
also be an immediate: an odd word that the foreign side encodes in the
handle itself and that is not a pointer.

The flag travels with the record (HIR `{ ffi tagged "path" }`, MIR
`{ "path", @hook, tagged }`) to the dialect type
`!reussir.ffi_object<"path", @hook, tagged = true>`, a default-valued
parameter, so existing builders and textual IR give an untagged object.
In the LLVM lowering, `rc.inc` updates the count and `rc.dec` calls the
cleanup hook only when the handle's low bit is clear; nothing is loaded
through the handle before the test. Untagged objects lower as before.

No other operation may access the count of a tagged object, and
`RcType::isTaggedFFIObject` is the one predicate for it. The verifier
rejects `rc.fetch`, `rc.fetch_sub`, `rc.set`, `rc.is_unique`,
`rc.assume_unique` and `rc.defer` on such a value. The passes that emit
them for an arbitrary rc value skip it: the nonlinear-FFI
instrumentation, the unique-carrying recursion analysis, and the worklist
drop expansion, which emits a plain `rc.dec` and, in the array task under
forward drop order, sends every element to its release function. The
checker and monomorphization reject `core::object::is_unique` on a tagged
record; `unsafe_inc` and `unsafe_dec` stay allowed.

The foreign type must be a pointer-sized scalar that promises neither
alignment nor dereferenceability: an integer or a raw pointer, not a
reference, a `Box`, or an `Option` of one, whose `align` attribute would
let LLVM fold the low-bit test. This is the declaration's contract. The
texture of the drop hook checks only what it can: the type has the size
and the alignment of `usize`.

An atomic tagged object cannot come from the frontend but is valid MLIR,
as an atomic untagged one is, so its paths are kept and tested.

Tests: basic/failure/tagged_ffi_count_ops.mlir (the verifier rule);
conversion/tagged_ffi_object.mlir, instrument_nonlinear_ffi_tagged.mlir,
worklist_tagged_ffi.mlir, worklist_tagged_ffi_array.mlir (no count access,
plain and atomic); frontend/ffi_tagged.rr (HIR, MIR, MLIR),
ffi_tagged_worklist.rr and ffi_tagged_array.rr (records, enums and arrays
of immediates run under both drop orders), ffi_tagged_is_unique.rr,
ffi_tagged_word.rr (`u8` refused); HIR and MIR round-trip unit
tests.
